{"error":false,"activeTest":false,"task":{"id":14243364,"type":"Review","status":"Processed","importance":1,"reason":null,"locked":null,"proofId":null,"currentQuestionId":null,"currentQuestionTimestamp":null,"progress":1,"points":4,"passed":1,"rawPointsAwarded":2,"pointsAwarded":2,"mp":null,"mpAwarded":null,"mpDecayRate":null,"student":{"id":57964,"firstName":"William","lastName":"Parker","locale":null,"subscriptionId":"sub_1UO2MGD4gcTFRx970s7DzjQk","sequenceId":15},"course":{"id":55,"name":"Linear Algebra"},"topic":{"id":1972,"name":"The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"},"test":{"id":null,"name":null,"diagnostic":null,"timeLimit":null,"questionCount":null},"created":"2026-10-15T13:58:14.000Z","started":"2026-10-15T14:48:08.000Z","completed":"2026-10-15T14:49:35.000Z","processed":"2026-10-15T14:49:46.000Z","progressStr":"100%","answers":[{"id":62275531,"created":"2026-10-15T14:48:23.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":15322,"number":1,"createdStr":"Thu, Oct 15th @ 2:48 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:15","question":{"id":38704,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":5510,"text":"<p>Given that $\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$</p>\n","choice1":"$\\dfrac{7}{6}$","choice2":"$\\dfrac{4}{3}$","choice3":"$-\\dfrac{1}{6}$","choice4":"$-3$","choice5":"$-\\dfrac{7}{6}$","freeAnswerChoice":" ","explanation":"<p>Let&#39;s find the characteristic equation $\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\t\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I) &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\\left|\\begin{array}{cc}\n\t\t3-\\lambda & 3 \\\\\n\t\t3 & 5-\\lambda\n\t\\end{array}\\right| &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\t(3-\\lambda)(5-\\lambda)-9 &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\\lambda^2-8\\lambda+6 &= 0\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tA^2-8A+6I &= O.\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Since $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tA^{-1}(A^2-8A+6I) &= O \\\\[5pt]\n\tA-8I+6A^{-1} &= O \\\\[5pt]\n\t6A^{-1} &= 8I-A \\\\[5pt]\n\tA^{-1} &= \\dfrac{4}{3}I-\\dfrac{1}{6}A\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Therefore, $p=\\dfrac{4}{3},$ $q=-\\dfrac{1}{6}$ and\n\\[\np+q=\\dfrac{4}{3}+\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{6}\\right)=\\dfrac{7}{6}.\n\\]</p>\n","topic":{"id":1972,"name":"The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"},"example":{"id":5510,"title":"Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"},"group":{"id":12822,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[{"id":null,"number":null,"answers":[]}],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14243364,"answer":{"id":62275531,"cread":"2026-10-15T14:48:23.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":15322},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62275548,"created":"2026-10-15T14:48:40.000Z","choices":null,"correct":0,"selectedChoice":4,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":13499,"number":2,"createdStr":"Thu, Oct 15th @ 2:48 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:13","question":{"id":38723,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":5511,"text":"<p>Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)=-\\lambda^3-2\\lambda-1.$ Then, $A^3=$</p>\n","choice1":"$-2A - I$","choice2":"$-2A^2 - I$","choice3":"$-2A^2 - A$","choice4":"$-2A + A^2$","choice5":"$2A - I$","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tp(A) &= O \\\\[3pt]\n\t-A^3-2A-I &= O \\\\[3pt]\n\tA^3 &= -2A - I.\n\\end{align}</p>\n","topic":{"id":1972,"name":"The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"},"example":{"id":5511,"title":"Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"},"group":{"id":12823,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14243364,"answer":{"id":62275548,"cread":"2026-10-15T14:48:40.000Z","choices":null,"correct":0,"selectedChoice":4,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":13499},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62275593,"created":"2026-10-15T14:48:58.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":14025,"number":3,"createdStr":"Thu, Oct 15th @ 2:48 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:14","question":{"id":38727,"format":"MC","difficulty":3,"exampleId":5512,"text":"<p>Given the matrix\n$\\,\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}\n\t5 & 0 \\\\\n\t10 & -5\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$\nand $f(x)=x^{23}-25x^{21}-4x+2$, find $f(A).$</p>\n","choice1":"$\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t-18 & 0 \\\\\n\t-40 & 22\n\\end{bmatrix}$","choice2":"$\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t18 & 0 \\\\\n\t40 & -22\n\\end{bmatrix}$","choice3":"$\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t-20 & 40 \\\\\n\t0 & 20\n\\end{bmatrix}$","choice4":"$\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t-10 & 40 \\\\\n\t0 & -10\n\\end{bmatrix}$","choice5":"$\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t-20 & 22 \\\\\n\t-42 & 20\n\\end{bmatrix}$","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>Let&#39;s start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)\\mathbin{:}$</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tp(\\lambda) &= \\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\[5pt]\n\t&= \\left|\\begin{array}{cc}\n\t5-\\lambda & 0 \\\\\n\t10 & -5-\\lambda\n\t\\end{array}\\right| \\\\[5pt]\n\t&= (5-\\lambda)(-5-\\lambda) \\\\[5pt]\n\t&= \\lambda^2-25\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Now, let&#39;s divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:</p>\n<table rules=all cellpadding=0 align=\"center\" style=\"border:hidden; border-color:black; margin: auto; margin-bottom: 4mm; margin-top: 4mm\">\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0;\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:left; border: hidden; padding: 0;\">$\\:\\:x^{21}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0;\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0;\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0;\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0\">$x^2-25\\:$</td>\n<td colspan=7 width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:left; border: hidden; padding: 0\">$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{23} \\: - \\: 25x^{21} \\: - \\: 4x \\: + \\: 2 {\\phantom{|}} }$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr style=\"border: 1.5px solid\">\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\">$x^{23}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\">$-$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\">$25x^{21}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\">$-$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\">$4x$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\">$+$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n\n</table>\n\n<p>Therefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\\begin{align}\n\tf(x) &= q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\[3pt]\n\t&= q(x)p(x)+(-4x+2)\n\\end{align}\nwhere $q(x) = x^{21}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have</p>\n<p>\\[\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(-4A+2I).\n\\]</p>\n<p>According to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\nf(A) \n&= q(A)p(A)+(-4A+2I) \\\\[5pt]\n&= q(A)\\cdot O+(-4A+2I) \\\\[5pt]\n\t&= -4A+2I \\\\[5pt]\n\t&=-4\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t5 & 0 \\\\\n\t10 & -5\n\\end{bmatrix} +\n2\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t1 & 0 \\\\\n\t0 & 1\n\\end{bmatrix} \\\\[5pt]\n\t&= \\begin{bmatrix}\n\t-20 & 0 \\\\\n\t-40 & 20\n\\end{bmatrix} +\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t2 & 0 \\\\\n\t0 & 2\n\\end{bmatrix} \\\\[5pt]\n\t&= \\begin{bmatrix}\n\t-18 & 0 \\\\\n\t-40 & 22\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\end{align}</p>\n","topic":{"id":1972,"name":"The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"},"example":{"id":5512,"title":"Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"},"group":{"id":12824,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14243364,"answer":{"id":62275593,"cread":"2026-10-15T14:48:58.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":14025},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyHard","difficultySymbol":"H","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62275641,"created":"2026-10-15T14:49:17.000Z","choices":null,"correct":0,"selectedChoice":2,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":15457,"number":4,"createdStr":"Thu, Oct 15th @ 2:49 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:15","question":{"id":85926,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":5511,"text":"<p>Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)=-\\lambda^3+2\\lambda^2+22.$ Then, $A^3=$</p>\n","choice1":"$2A^2 + 22I$","choice2":"$2A^2 - 22I$","choice3":"$22A^2 + 2I$","choice4":"$2A + 22I$","choice5":"$2A - 22I$","freeAnswerChoice":" ","explanation":"<p>Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tp(A) &= O \\\\[3pt]\n\t-A^3+2A^2+22I &= O \\\\[3pt]\n\tA^3 &= 2A^2 + 22I.\n\\end{align}</p>\n","topic":{"id":1972,"name":"The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"},"example":{"id":5511,"title":"Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"},"group":{"id":12823,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14243364,"answer":{"id":62275641,"cread":"2026-10-15T14:49:17.000Z","choices":null,"correct":0,"selectedChoice":2,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":15457},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62275664,"created":"2026-10-15T14:49:34.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":12603,"number":5,"createdStr":"Thu, Oct 15th @ 2:49 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:13","question":{"id":85993,"format":"MC","difficulty":3,"exampleId":5512,"text":"<p>Given the matrix\n$\\,\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}\n\t4 & 12 \\\\\n\t0 & -4\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$\nand $f(x)=x^{21}-16x^{19}+6x-3$, find $f(A).$</p>\n","choice1":"$\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t21 & 72 \\\\\n\t0 & -27\n\\end{bmatrix}$","choice2":"$\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t27 & 72 \\\\\n\t0 & -21\n\\end{bmatrix}$","choice3":"$\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t21 & 27 \\\\\n\t0 & -72\n\\end{bmatrix}$","choice4":"$\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t12 & 72 \\\\\n\t0 & -12\n\\end{bmatrix}$","choice5":"$\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t21 & 0 \\\\\n\t72 & -27\n\\end{bmatrix}$","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>Let&#39;s start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)\\mathbin{:}$</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tp(\\lambda) &= \\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\[5pt]\n\t&= \\left|\\begin{array}{cc}\n\t4-\\lambda & 12 \\\\\n\t0 & -4-\\lambda\n\t\\end{array}\\right| \\\\[5pt]\n\t&= (4-\\lambda)(-4-\\lambda) \\\\[5pt]\n\t&= \\lambda^2-16\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Now, let&#39;s divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:</p>\n<table rules=all cellpadding=0 align=\"center\" style=\"border:hidden; border-color:black; margin: auto; margin-bottom: 4mm; margin-top: 4mm\">\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0;\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:left; border: hidden; padding: 0;\">$\\:\\:x^{19}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0;\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0;\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0;\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0\">$x^2-16\\:$</td>\n<td colspan=7 width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:left; border: hidden; padding: 0\">$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{21} \\: - \\: 16x^{19} \\: + \\: 6x \\:\\, - \\:\\, 3 {\\phantom{|}} }$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr style=\"border: 1.5px solid\">\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\">$x^{21}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\">$-$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\">$16x^{19}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-top: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\">$6x$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\">$-$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" style=\"text-align:center; border-right: hidden; border-right: hidden; padding: 0\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n\n</table>\n\n<p>Therefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\\begin{align}\n\tf(x) &= q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\[3pt]\n\t&= q(x)p(x)+(6x-3)\n\\end{align}\nwhere $q(x) = x^{19}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have</p>\n<p>\\[\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I).\n\\]</p>\n<p>According to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\nf(A) \n&= q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I) \\\\[5pt]\n&= q(A)\\cdot O+(6A-3I) \\\\[5pt]\n\t&= 6A-3I \\\\[5pt]\n\t&=6\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t4 & 12 \\\\\n\t0 & -4\n\\end{bmatrix} -\n3\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t1 & 0 \\\\\n\t0 & 1\n\\end{bmatrix} \\\\[5pt]\n\t&= \\begin{bmatrix}\n\t24 & 72 \\\\\n\t0 & -24\n\\end{bmatrix} -\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\t3 & 0 \\\\\n\t0 & 3\n\\end{bmatrix} \\\\[5pt]\n\t&= \\begin{bmatrix}\n\t21 & 72 \\\\\n\t0 & -27\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\end{align}</p>\n","topic":{"id":1972,"name":"The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"},"example":{"id":5512,"title":"Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"},"group":{"id":12824,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14243364,"answer":{"id":62275664,"cread":"2026-10-15T14:49:34.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":12603},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyHard","difficultySymbol":"H","rendered":true,"testMode":false}}],"completedStr":"Completed @ 2:49 PM","localGraph":{"topicId":1972,"topics":[{"id":427,"name":"Dividing Polynomials Using Long Division","prerequisites":[],"frontier":0,"halfLife":1.259496499575261,"repetition":2,"stability":0.17590079880424395},{"id":729,"name":"Vieta's Formulas","prerequisites":[],"frontier":0,"halfLife":999999999.0000005,"repetition":29,"stability":1},{"id":1725,"name":"Powers of Matrices","prerequisites":[],"frontier":0,"halfLife":216.40056693484928,"repetition":7,"stability":1},{"id":1964,"name":"The Characteristic Equation of a Matrix","prerequisites":[],"frontier":0,"halfLife":222.5800718743089,"repetition":7,"stability":1},{"id":1972,"name":"The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem","prerequisites":[427,729,1725,1964],"frontier":0,"halfLife":0.7883229457417311,"repetition":1,"stability":0.14545949254856955},{"id":6375,"name":"Matrix Exponentials","prerequisites":[1972],"frontier":0,"halfLife":0.3358125896833679,"repetition":1,"stability":0.09845079191203376}]}}}